> I'm making a shiny app, but when I wrap my page in a page_navbar, and have this be one nav_panel within it, suddenly the bootstrapPage that I'm using doesn't work any more. The columns show up vertically.
**assistant**:
> It sounds like you're experiencing issues with layout and styling when combining `page_navbar` with `bootstrapPage`. The `page_navbar` function is part of the newer layout system in Shiny and may not be fully compatible with the old `bootstrapPage` layout functions. The `page_navbar` creates a Navbar page that is designed to work with Bootstrap 4, whereas `bootstrapPage` might be expecting Bootstrap 3.
> 
> To resolve the layout problem, I would recommend using the newer layout functions consistently. Here's how you can structure your app using `page_navbar` and appropriate replacements:
> 
> 1. Replace `bootstrapPage` with `fluidPage`, which is compatible with the navbar layout.
> 
> 2. Make sure you're using the responsive grid system with `fluidRow` and `column` to position your elements, and they should align correctly within the `nav_panel`.
> 
> Here's a basic example structure:
> 
> ```r
> library(shiny)
> 
> ui <- page_navbar(
>   title = "My Shiny App",
>   
>   nav_panel(
>     "Home",
>     fluidPage(
>       fluidRow(
>         column(4, wellPanel("Content in Column 1")),
>         column(4, wellPanel("Content in Column 2")),
>         column(4, wellPanel("Content in Column 3"))
>       )
>     )
>   ),
>   
>   nav_panel(
>     "Another Tab",
>     fluidPage(
>       # Additional content for this tab
>     )
>   )
> )
> 
> server <- function(input, output, session) {
>   # Server logic here
> }
> 
> shinyApp(ui, server)
> ```
> 
> Ensure you are structuring your elements within `fluidPage`, using `fluidRow` and `column` to help position items horizontally instead of vertically. Let me know if this helps or if you need further assistance!
