> How do I do a fluidPage with a sidebarPanel and a main content part? Or is that even the right function to use now?
**assistant**:
> Yes, you can certainly use `fluidPage` to create an app layout with a `sidebarPanel` and a main content area! This layout is appropriate for many Shiny applications where you want a sidebar for inputs and controls and a main area for displaying outputs or results.
> 
> Here’s how you can set it up:
> 
> 1. Use `fluidPage` to define the overall layout.
> 2. Inside it, use `sidebarLayout` which allows you to specify both a `sidebarPanel` and a `mainPanel`.
> 
> Here's a basic example:
> 
> ```r
> library(shiny)
> 
> ui <- fluidPage(
>   titlePanel("My Shiny App with Sidebar"),
>   
>   sidebarLayout(
>     sidebarPanel(
>       sliderInput("num",
>                   "Choose a number:",
>                   min = 1,
>                   max = 100,
>                   value = 50),
>       textInput("text", "Enter some text:")
>     ),
>     
>     mainPanel(
>       h3("Your Inputs:"),
>       textOutput("textOutput"),
>       textOutput("numOutput")
>     )
>   )
> )
> 
> server <- function(input, output) {
>   output$textOutput <- renderText({
>     input$text
>   })
>   
>   output$numOutput <- renderText({
>     input$num
>   })
> }
> 
> shinyApp(ui, server)
> ```
> 
> ### Explanation:
> - **`fluidPage`**: It creates a fluid layout that adapts to the size of the browser window.
> - **`sidebarLayout`**: This function creates a layout with a sidebar and a main area.
> - **`sidebarPanel`**: Contains input controls (like `sliderInput` and `textInput` in this example).
> - **`mainPanel`**: This is where you would display output, like text or plots.
> 
> This structure allows you to create a responsive and user-friendly interface. If you find that you need additional navigation features, consider integrating with `page_navbar` or other layout functions if desired. Let me know if you need more help with this or any other features!
